  ],
  "notes": "Roles are identified by trust principal and managed policy ARNs, not names: controllers by EC2 plus EKS pods (or web identity), nodes by EC2 only with the three worker policies, control plane by EC2 only without them. Attachment may be written on either side (ManagedPolicy Roles or role ManagedPolicyArns). Only AWS::IAM::ManagedPolicy counts as customer managed. A template whose controllers or nodes role does not have the stated trust or policies can't have that role identified, so its attach checks fail too. Real templates: GLM53F's controllers role trusts eks.amazonaws.com rather than EKS pods and its nodes role has no worker policies; OpenCode_DSV4F's controllers role trusts EC2 only and its nodes role gets inline copies rather than the AWS managed policies. Those failures are kept. The four permission-set checks require the customer managed policy to be attached (either side) to an EC2-assumable role, so an unattached policy fails; which specific role gets it is the detail attach_* checks. instance_profiles_ref_roles requires profiles for the controllers role and for an EC2-only role (control plane or nodes); with instance_profiles_distinct_roles that gives three distinct roles."
}
